<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  ONE-SHOT. pg_repack the `videos` heap after scripts/null-video-text.ts has cleared the
  description / metadata / llm_summary columns.

  DISABLED. Two of the four preconditions are now met; two are not.

    1. NOT MET — the null-out has not run, and when it does (06:00, com.mfm.video-scripter-
       null-video-text) it clears llm_summary ONLY. description still has 10 direct readers and
       metadata 18, so those two columns keep their text. description averages 1,038 bytes of a
       2,065-byte row and metadata 742; llm_summary is 210. Repacking now would therefore
       reclaim the llm_summary dead space and almost nothing else — a few hundred MB of a
       3,984 MB table. THE REPACK IS NOT WORTH A NIGHT UNTIL description IS CLEARED TOO.
    2. MET — the client binary exists. scripts/docker/pg-repack.Dockerfile builds pg_repack
       1.5.2 from source against PostgreSQL 15; no packaged 1.5.2 client exists for this
       machine (brew has no formula, apt has only 1.5.3 and 1.4.8, and the local psql is 14.17
       against a 15.8 server). pg_repack refuses to run on a version mismatch.
    3. MET — the extension is created. `create extension pg_repack` SUCCEEDS as the `postgres`
       role on this Supabase instance, which is NOT a true superuser (pg_user.usesuper = false).
       It does not need the dashboard. Installed 2026-09-14, version 1.5.2, schema public.
    4. NOT VERIFIED — free disk for a second copy. The database is 10 GB of an 18 GB volume and
       `videos` is 3,984 MB. Check again on the night, after the null-out.

  CANARY, 2026-09-14: repacked public.channel_stats through this exact image and flag set.
  2,680 kB -> 1,728 kB (36 % reclaimed), 6,683 rows before and after, about 12 seconds of which
  most was spent waiting for one open transaction to finish. Online throughout; nothing blocked.

  Nulling the columns does NOT shrink the file — it frees space inside the heap. This is the
  step that returns it to the filesystem.

  Why pg_repack and not VACUUM FULL: pg_repack rewrites online, taking ACCESS EXCLUSIVE only
  briefly at the start and at the swap. VACUUM FULL holds it for the WHOLE rewrite — tens of
  minutes on a 1,734 MB heap at 5-23 ms/block, during which every read and write of `videos`
  blocks: the app, the RSS poller, the scorer, the classification workers. Do not schedule it.

  Prepare:
    docker build -t pg-repack:1.5.2-pg15 -f scripts/docker/pg-repack.Dockerfile scripts/docker
    docker run --rm pg-repack:1.5.2-pg15 pg_repack --version    # must print exactly 1.5.2
    npx tsx scripts/null-video-text.ts --verify

  Install, run once, watch, then remove — on a night that is NOT the null-out's night:
    cp scripts/launchd/com.mfm.video-scripter-repack-videos.plist ~/Library/LaunchAgents/
    launchctl bootstrap gui/$UID ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.mfm.video-scripter-repack-videos.plist
    launchctl kickstart -p gui/$UID/com.mfm.video-scripter-repack-videos
    tail -f logs/repack-videos.log
    launchctl bootout gui/$UID/com.mfm.video-scripter-repack-videos
    rm ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.mfm.video-scripter-repack-videos.plist

  Interrupting it is safe: it builds a copy and swaps at the end, so the original is untouched.
  Clean up after a kill with `drop schema repack cascade;`.
-->
<plist version="1.0"><dict>
  <key>Label</key><string>com.mfm.video-scripter-repack-videos</string>
  <key>Disabled</key><true/>
  <key>EnvironmentVariables</key>
  <dict><key>PATH</key><string>/opt/homebrew/bin:/usr/bin:/bin</string></dict>
  <key>ProgramArguments</key>
  <array>
    <string>/bin/sh</string>
    <string>-c</string>
    <!--
      DATABASE_SESSION_URL (:5432), not DATABASE_URL (:6543). pg_repack holds advisory locks and
      temporary state across statements on ONE session; the transaction-mode pooler hands the
      connection to someone else between statements and the repack fails or corrupts its own
      bookkeeping.

      -k is the SHORT FORM of --no-superuser-check, not a companion to it. Passing both is what
      this line used to do, and pg_repack rejects it outright: "option -k, --no-superuser-check
      should be specified only once". The flag is needed because Supabase's `postgres` role is
      not a true superuser.

      Run through the container: no 1.5.2 client can be installed on the host (see
      scripts/docker/pg-repack.Dockerfile), and pg_repack will not run against a 1.5.2 extension
      with any other client version. --network host is not needed; Supabase is reached over the
      internet from inside the container.
    -->
    <string>set -a; . ./.env.local; set +a; exec docker run --rm pg-repack:1.5.2-pg15 pg_repack -k -d "$DATABASE_SESSION_URL" -t public.videos --wait-timeout 300 --elevel=INFO</string>
  </array>
  <key>WorkingDirectory</key><string>/Users/brandoncullum/video-scripter-v2/video-scripter</string>
  <key>StartCalendarInterval</key><dict><key>Hour</key><integer>6</integer><key>Minute</key><integer>30</integer></dict>
  <key>Nice</key><integer>10</integer>
  <key>ProcessType</key><string>Background</string>
  <key>LowPriorityIO</key><true/>
  <key>StandardOutPath</key><string>/Users/brandoncullum/video-scripter-v2/video-scripter/logs/repack-videos.log</string>
  <key>StandardErrorPath</key><string>/Users/brandoncullum/video-scripter-v2/video-scripter/logs/repack-videos.err.log</string>
</dict></plist>
